# Add to <product-repo>/.github/CODEOWNERS
#
# Decided 2026-08-03: the SOUP configuration stays in the product repo, and CODEOWNERS is what
# keeps the two QMS determinations in it from being changed in a feature PR.
#
# What is being protected, concretely. Without an owner on .soup-policy.yml, a one-line change
# moves a real obligation:
#
#   maintenance_interval        every Track 3/4 remediation deadline moves with it, because the
#   60d -> 180d                 deadline is the next window on that grid
#   cra_scope: true -> false    the KEV alert stops saying that a 24-hour reporting clock is
#                               running (CRA reporting obligations apply from 2026-09-11)
#
# validate-policy.sh checks that these values are *valid*, not that they are the ones QM
# determined — it cannot, because it has no second source to compare against. The review is
# therefore the control.
#
# IMPORTANT: CODEOWNERS is decoration unless branch protection on the default branch has
# "Require review from Code Owners" enabled. Without that, the file assigns reviewers and
# nothing enforces them. Check it per repo.

# --- QMS determinations: the SLA values and cra_scope -----------------------
.soup-policy.yml        @QuickBirdEng/qm

# --- risk acceptances, revised deadlines, vendor requests (§3.3, §3.5.1) ----
# A recorded acceptance of a missed deadline on a medical device should not be self-mergeable.
.soup-decisions.yml     @QuickBirdEng/soup-approvers

# --- scope is a statement about the code and is reviewed with it ------------
# Deliberately NOT owned by QM: it has to change in the same PR that adds a Dockerfile or a
# dependency, and routing that through QM would make the scope drift instead.
# .soup-scope.yml       (no owner — normal code review)
